{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789969547,"nonce":"0ZhNcGLwH0kiVOOjX+pyQMGOIMs=","title":"Python: a generator expression evaluates its outermost iterable lazily","message":"Python evaluates the outermost iterable of a generator expression when the\nexpression is created; only the element, the conditions and the inner\niterables run on iteration. The frontend lifts the whole expression into\nthe fiber (lower.rs generator_expr: child.comprehension over all of\ng.generators), so the first iterable is read when the generator is first\ndriven, after any rebinding of the name in between.\n\nReproducer (/tmp/p4-genexp.py on the NUC):\n\n    xs = [1, 2, 3]\n    g = (v * 2 for v in xs)\n    xs = [9]\n    print(list(g))\n\n    def f(n):\n        ys = [n, n + 1]\n        h = (y + 1 for y in ys)\n        ys = [100]\n        return list(h)\n\n    print(f(5))\n\npython3 prints [2, 4, 6] and [6, 7]; zypy at dea6d08b prints [18] and [101].\n\nFix shape: evaluate g.generators[0].iter in the enclosing scope, bind it to\na hidden local, and pass that local into the fiber as the first loop's\nsource (the same eager binding the rebind rewrite in rebind.rs relies on\nwhen it treats a genexp's outermost iterable as a plain occurrence).","files":null}]}